{{smaller|Specifically, in ''metal.'', the converter in which Bessemer steel is made. See ''steel''<sup>1</sup>.}}
{{smaller block|As far as my observation goes, metallurgical writers almost invariably use the word converter, while in the steel works the word ''vessel'' is almost always used.
{{right|''H. M. Howe'', Metal. of Steel, p. 339.}}}}

'''2.''' A ship; a craft of any kind: usually a larger craft than a boat, but in law often construed to mean any floating structure.
{{smaller block|<poem>Let ’s to the seaside, ho!
As well to see the ''vessel'' that ’s come in
As to throw out our eyes for brave Othello.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Othello, ii. 1. 37.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>He sent it with a small ''vessel''
That there was quickly gaun to sea.</poem>
{{right|''John Thomson and the Turk'' (Child’s Ballads, III. 353).}}}}

'''3.''' In ''anat.'' and ''zoöl.'', any duct or canal in which a fluid, as blood or lymph, is secreted, contained, or conveyed, as an artery, vein, capillary, lymphatic, or spermatic; especially, a blood-vessel. A part or organ pervaded or well provided with vessels is said to be ''vascular.'' —'''4.''' In ''bot.'', same as ''duct''—that is, a row of cells which have lost their intervening partitions, and consequently form a long continuous canal. {{smaller|The walls of the vessel or duct may be variously marked by pits, or by spiral, annular, or reticulated thickenings.}}

'''5.''' Figuratively, something conceived as formed to receive or contain; hence, especially in Scriptural phraseology, a person into whom anything is conceived as poured or infused, or to whom something has been imparted; a recipient.

{{smaller block|He is a chosen ''vessel'' unto me, to bear my name before}}

readings.c1s2:
{{smaller block|the Gentiles, and kings, and the children of Israel.
{{right|Acts ix. 15.}}}}
{{smaller block|What if God, willing to shew his wrath, and to make his power known, endured with much long-suffering the ''vessels'' of wrath fitted to destruction?{{right|Rom. ix. 22.}}}}
'''6†.''' Vessels collectively; plate.
{{smaller block|The ''vessel'' of the temple he with him ladde.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Monk’s Tale, l. 158.}}}}
{{smaller block|‘Goth, bringeth forth the ''vesseulx,''” quod he.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Monk’s Tale, l. 204.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Of gold ther is a borde, & tretels ther bi,
Of siluer other ''vesselle'' gilte fulle richeli.</poem>
{{right|''Rob. of Brunne'', p. 152.}}}}
'''Acoustic, ambulacral, annular, ascending, blind, capillary, cardiac, coronary, dorsal, gluteal, intercostal vessel.''' {{smaller|See the adjectives.}}—'''Lacteal vessels,''' {{smaller|lymphatics which absorb chyle from the intestinal canal. See ''lacteal'', ''n.''}}—'''Laticiferous, lymphatic, Malpighian, merchant vessel.''' {{smaller|See the adjectives.}}—'''Milk vessel.''' {{smaller|See ''milk-vessel.''}}—'''Obliterated vessel.''' {{smaller|See ''obliterate.''}}—'''Scalariform, spiral, umbilical,''' etc., '''vessel.''' {{smaller|See the adjectives.}}—'''Squeezed-in vessel.''' {{smaller|See ''squeeze.''}}—'''The weaker vessel,''' {{smaller|a phrase applied, now often jocularly, to a woman, in allusion to 1 Pet. iii. 7: “Giving honour unto the wife as unto ''the weaker vessel.''”}}
{{smaller block|I must comfort ''the weaker vessel,'' as doublet and hose ought to show itself courageous to petticoat.
{{right|''Shak.'', As you Like it, ii. 4. 6.}}}}

