'''vessel†''' (ves′el), ''v. t.'' [< ME. ''vesselen''; < ''vessel'', ''n.''] To put into a vessel.
{{smaller block|<poem>Aloes tweyne unces epatike;
Let ''vessel'' it, and set it uppe in smyke.</poem>
{{right|''Palladius'', Husbondrie (E. E. T. S.), p. 200.}}}}
{{smaller block|Take that earth and . . . ''vessel'' it, and in that . . . set the seed.{{right|''Bacon'', Nat. Hist., § 529.}}}}

'''vesselful''' (ves′el-fu̇l), ''n.'' [< ''vessel'' + ''-ful.''] As much as a vessel will hold.

readings.c2s1:
An article of clothing covering the person; an outer garment; a vestment. [Archaic.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Over his lucid arms
A military ''vest'' of purple flow’d.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., xi. 241.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>The rivets of the ''vest''
Which girds in steel his ample breast.</poem>
{{right|''Whittier'', Mogg Megone, iii.}}}}
'''2.''' Figuratively, garment; dress; array; vesture.
{{smaller block|<poem>Not seldom, clad in radiant ''vest'',
Deceitfully goes forth the morn.</poem>
{{right|''Wordsworth'', Near the Spring of the Hermitage.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Wherever he be flown, whatever ''vest''
The being hath put on which lately here
So many-friended was.</poem>
{{right|''Lowell'', Agassiz, vi. 2.}}}}
'''3.''' A body-garment for men’s wear, at different times of distinct types. {{smaller|(''a'') Originally, a garment like a cassock, said by Pepys to have been adopted by Charles II. as the fashion for his court, and ridiculed by Louis XIV. of France, who put his servants into such vests.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>You are not to learn,
At these Years, how absolutely necessary a rich ''Vest''
And a Perruque are to a Man that aims at their [ladies’]
Favours.</poem>
{{right|''Etherege'', She Would if she Could, iii. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|The ''vest'' is gathered up before them [figures on medals] like an apron, which you must suppose filled with fruits as well as the cornu-copiæ. ''Addison'', Ancient Medals, ii.}}
{{smaller block|Under his doublet Charles appeared in a ''vest'', “being a long cassock,” as Pepys explains, “close to the body, of black cloth and pinked with white silk under it.”
{{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', VI. 473.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') A body-garment of later times; especially, the waistcoat in the ordinary modern sense — that is, a short garment without sleeves, buttoning down the front, and hav-}}

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller|ing the back concealed by the coat.}}
{{smaller block|Numerous pegs with coats and “pants” and “''vests''”—as he was in the habit of calling waistcoats and pantaloons or trousers—hanging up as if the owner had melted out of them.
{{right|''O. W. Holmes'', Professor, vii.}}
If tailors would only print upon waistcoats, I would give double price for a ''vest'' bearing this inscription.
{{right|''R. D. Blackmore'', Lorna Doone, lxvi.}}}}
'''4.''' An outer garment, or part of such a garment, for women. {{smaller|Especially—(''a'') A sort of jacket with or without sleeves, and known by many different names according to changing fashion: as, Breton ''vest'', Oriental ''vest'', etc. (''b'') A trimming or facing of the front of the bodice, sometimes with a different material, and following more or less closely the form of a man’s vest: a fashion often reappearing. Over the vest of this form a coat is generally worn.}}
'''5.''' An undergarment knitted or woven on the stocking-loom. ''Vest'' and ''undervest'' are more common in England; ''undershirt'' in the United States.

'''vest''' (vest), ''v.'' [< OF. ''vestir'', F. ''vêtir'' = Sp. Pg. ''vestir'' = It. ''vestire'', < L. ''vestire'', clothe, dress, < ''vestis'', a garment, clothing: see ''vest'', ''n.'' Cf. ''wear''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To clothe with or as with a garment, vest, or vestment; robe; dress; cover, surround, or encompass closely.
{{smaller block|''Vested'' all in white, pure as her mind.
{{right|''Milton'', Sonnets, xviii.}}}}
'''2.''' To invest or clothe, as with authority; put in possession (of); endow; put more or less formally in occupation (of): followed by ''with''.
{{smaller block|To settle men’s consciences, ’tis necessary that they know the person who by right is ''vested with'' power over them.
{{right|''Locke.''}}
<poem>Had I been ''vested with'' the Monarch’s Pow’r,</poem>}}

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|Thou must have sigh’d, unlucky Youth, in vain.
{{right|''Prior'', To Mr. Howard.}}}}
'''3.''' To place or put in possession or at the disposal of; give or confer formally or legally an immediate fixed right of present or future possession, occupancy, or enjoyment of; commit to: followed by ''in''.

{{smaller block|So, instead of getting licenses in mortmain to enable him to ''vest'' his lands ''in'' the Gild of the Holy Cross, he made a deed of feoffment, ''vesting'' them ''in'' persons therein named. {{right|''English Gilds'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 252.}}}}

{{smaller block|I will not trust executive power, ''vested in'' the hands of a single magistrate, to keep the vigils of liberty.
{{right|''D. Webster'', Speech, Senate, May 7, 1834.}}}}
'''4.''' To lay out, as money or capital; invest: as, to ''vest'' money in land. [Rare.] ''Imp. Dict.''

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To put on clothing or vestments.

{{smaller block|Even in the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries it was the common custom for priests, at least in England, to ''vest'' in the sanctuary. {{right|''Cath. Dict.'', p. 838.}}}}
'''2.''' To come or descend; devolve; take effect, as a title or right: with ''in''.

{{smaller block|The supreme power could not be said to ''vest in'' them exclusively. {{right|''Brougham.''}}}}

{{smaller block|It is already the usage to speak of a trust as a thing that ''vests'', and as a thing that may be divested.
{{right|''Bentham'', Introd. to Morals and Legislation, xvi. 27, note.}}}}

'''To vest in interest''', {{smaller|to pass or devolve as matter of right or title irrespective of any immediate right of possession.}}—'''To vest in possession''', {{smaller|to pass in possession or immediate right of possession. See ''vested''.}}

